Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 21:07:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 64/76] rc3-instr 8/8: bound the GDN recurrent state index, and
 prove the telemetry  survives replay

Two things the round-2 review asked for (ASTRA-GUARDS-R2.md R2-6 and
qualification item 3).

THE GUARD. fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule_fwd_kernel carries the identical
unbounded `i_t = num_accepted_tokens - 1` that commit 3/8 fixed in
fused_sigmoid_gating -- same module, same shape, same upstream PR #50021 hunk
(pr50021.diff:548-585). Round 2 left it alone and dropped the recurrent arm of
the ported upstream test rather than assert a fix not made, which was honest
but left a hole: a clean rc3 boot would have cleared the GDN decode-UPDATE
kernel only, and the release note would have had to say so. The review's
judgement was that the port is cheaper than the caveat, and it is: the hunk is
a text-match apply of a shape already done twice.

So: the load is masked to the row, `other=0` falls into the existing
invalid-state path, and that path now zero-fills its output rows instead of
returning with `o` (a `q.new_empty`) holding allocator residue. A new telemetry
site, SITE_GDN_RECURRENT_STATE, records hits.

THE REPLAY TEST. tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_guard_telemetry_replay.py. This is
the single test that distinguishes "B1 architecturally fixed" from "B1 fixed".

Round 1's telemetry was dead under CUDA-graph replay: a per-launch counter and
an inline readback, in kernels that sit inside the captured forward. Round 2
rebuilt it on a persistent buffer and captured atomics, and every argument
since has been about code rather than measurement. This measures it -- capture
a graph around a guarded kernel whose guard MUST fire (num_accepted_tokens = 0,
so i_t == -1), replay it five times without re-entering Python, and require the
counter to have advanced five launches' worth. A delta of zero is B1, unfixed.

The test deliberately establishes its own control first: it asserts the seeded
fault fires EAGERLY before it trusts anything the replay says, because a silent
control would make the replay assertion meaningless either way. A second case
pins the counter's data_ptr across calls, including through the unindexed
torch.device("cuda") alias (review R2-7) -- if counter() ever returned a fresh
tensor, the captured graph would write to a freed address, which is the half of
B1 that corrupts memory rather than merely losing evidence.

Both are CUDA-gated: +2 skips on CPU (47 -> 49). Neither has run.

ROUND 5 (review R3-4). The B2-class launch-site sweep, re-run for this kernel,
found a SECOND launch: flash_linear_attention/ops/kda.py:71 imports
fused_recurrent_gated_delta_rule_fwd_kernel from fused_recurrent.py and
launches it itself. This commit adds a required guard_ctr parameter, so that
launch would have died with a missing argument the first time a KDA layer ran.
Now updated, with the same telemetry site -- the defect being guarded is the
kernel's, not the caller's. That is the third time in this series a changed
signature had a launch site the first pass missed.

The KDA siblings named in the same upstream PR (pr50021.diff:481-547,
models/kimi_k3/{amd,nvidia}/ops/third_party/kda/fused_recurrent.py, plus the
glm5next pair) are SEPARATE COPIES of the kernel in their own files, not
callers of this one. They carry the same defect and are NOT fixed here; they
are off this model's path. Stated rather than omitted.

Draft for Astra review. Not merged into any RC.
